Vincent Huang <vincent9@ms3.hinet.net> wrote:
}Hello!
}I use DU4.0B OS in my dec1000a server,
}and I found one directory called "proc" in  root directory

   If you do "man 4 proc" it'll explain a lot.

}somebody said that these files in  the  /proc directory means the total 
}process running in this environment ,if this point of view is right.
}here is my questions....
}1). What does it mean about the size of one file in /proc directory ,
}      the space of that process occuppied in RAM? or...?

     The "size" of the file is the size of the process's address space
     (and the filename is the pid).  Example:

lambda.cc> ls -l /proc/01314
-rw-------   1 root     system   8806400 Nov 21 01:22 /proc/01314
lambda.cc> ps l01314
  UID   PID  PPID  CP PRI  NI   VSZ  RSS WCHAN    S    TTY             TIME COM
    0  1314     1   0  42   0  800K   0K 6b8f98   U    ??           0:09.70 /sb


}2). why the usauge of /proc always to be 100%

     The proc filesystem is not real, it takes no actual space.
lambda.cc> df -k -t procfs
Filesystem  1024-blocks        Used   Available Capacity  Mounted on
/proc                 0           0           0   100%    /proc

     I'd have called 0 of 0 0%, but they called it 100%,
     but it makes little difference.

}3). the total size of these files in /proc equal to the size of RAM?

     No (see #1).

}4). those process in swap space would also show up in /proc directory?

     Yup.  All processes, even swapped ones:

lambda.cc> ps l15735
  UID   PID  PPID  CP PRI  NI   VSZ  RSS WCHAN    S    TTY             TIME COM
    0 15735     1   0  44   0  432K   0K ttyin    IW + console      0:00.06 /us
lambda.cc> ls -l /proc/15735
-rw-------   1 root     system   8642560 Nov 21 01:25 /proc/15735


