We have a problem with loss of swap space when using deferred (lazy) mode
of swap. When we run a program, we can see that some swap space is
allocated and after graceful termination on the program, the swap space
is not returned. Eventually, the machine runs out of swap space and
crashes.

This problem is experienced on machines running Digital UNIX v3.2c,
v3.2d, v3.2g and v4.0b. It seems that the problem is not apparent when no
swap space is used, i.e. on machines with lots of physical memory.

In addition, the problem is not associated with our applications, as I
used a program which just has a static array of integers which it assigns
values to a couple of times to get the problem to appear on a machine
with only the O/S loaded.

If you run the same program on a machine which uses immediate mode for
swap, the swap space increases as expected during execution and returns
back to the previous value after graceful termination.

Any ideas?

Regards
Jacques

